(Discrepancy 1 (NAM), companion -- RESOLVED (vox #65), re-recorded
 2026-08-22 against vox 0.4.9. Used to demonstrate that D1.vox's
 garbage value was not a fixed sentinel and not derived from the
 string's content -- it was the ADDRESS of that declaration's own
 string literal in the compiled binary (two declarations of the
 identical string "abc" printed two DIFFERENT values, ruling out both
 "a hash of the content" and "a fixed constant"). That whole failure
 mode is gone: every one of these five declarations (four `number`,
 one `boolean`) is now individually rejected at compile time, the same
 way D1.vox's single case is -- matching the reassignment form
 (variables.md VAR-26, "cannot assign text to 'n', which is a
 number"). Compiler reports all five errors in one pass; only the
 first is checked here.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D1b.vox -o p
